There are 37 routes throughout the state.  Each route is 50-80 miles and i=
s suppose to be representative of the county or surrounding area.  The ro=
ute is driven in one direction one day and then driven in the opposite di=
rection within 7 days.  The observers are instructed to stop and "glass" a=
n area when geese are observed.  The total number of pairs and total numb=
er of geese are recorded at each site along the route.  We then calculate=
 geese per mile and pairs per mile.  I would like to incorporate distance=
 sampling into the survey.

My questions:
1)  Is this a valid use of distance sampling?
2)If there are several flocks and pairs spread out across a body of water=
, do we take the distance to the middle of the pond?
3) If the geese are strung out along a shoreline what distance do we meas=
ure?
4)If the geese are too far for a rangefinder can we estimate the distance=
?
5)Do we need to run the route twice?=20

Thank you very much for your help,
Faye

